\section{Test an Expression by Moving Something}

Replace \verb|"moss"| with \verb|"fernleaf"|. The difference becomes eight, the emitted integer becomes seventeen, and the relocation site moves from nine to thirteen. Its target is still the second byte in the payload, so its addend stays one. As a negative test, switch to \texttt{.data} just before \texttt{limit}. Now \verb|limit - packet| no longer cancels a base, and GNU \texttt{as} rejects the expression in this fixture rather than writing a fictitious length. These tests distinguish a value change, a change in the site of a reference, and a change in whether the expression can be evaluated at all.

The invariant is that absolute expressions are unchanged by rebasing sections without otherwise changing their internal layout. A relocatable expression must carry every surviving placement dependency in a form the ABI can encode. Neither a lexer classifying a token as an identifier nor an early provisional offset decides that question on its own.

There is a separate check at the point of use: representability as an expression does not guarantee a fit in the chosen storage field. A count of nine is absolute and fits in a four-byte field; a much larger absolute count might not. Conversely, even a label currently at offset zero can require a full address field. Expression evaluation should not silently narrow values just to make a later encoding check succeed.
